{
  "subtype": "what_if",
  "outcome_variable": "resolution_minutes",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "issue_category",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The type of issue is a common cause: it drives which resolution action is chosen and also directly affects how long resolution takes. It is an intrinsic property of the ticket (not a settable lever), so it functions as a confounder rather than a treatment. Not named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "requires_external_action",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Whether the ticket is awaiting engineering/infra/customer is largely a downstream consequence of the issue and the chosen handling path, and it lies on the causal pathway to resolution time; it is a mediator/collider-like state rather than a common cause of an intervenable treatment."
    },
    {
      "name": "external_dependency_type",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The specific external dependency (identity provider, cloud infra, etc.) is a consequence of the issue type and sits on the pathway delaying resolution; it is a mediator, not an adjustable confounder or lever."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_action",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "The action taken to resolve the ticket (investigation, config flag toggle, documentation, workaround, etc.) is something a support team can actually SET or DECIDE, making it an intervenable treatment for the what-if question about resolution time."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["resolution_action"],
  "treatment_intervenable": true,
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "issue_category", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.7}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.4,
  "rationale": "The set contains one genuine intervenable treatment (resolution_action) that a support team could actually choose, satisfying part (a). For part (b), only issue_category functions as a true confounder — a common cause of both the chosen resolution action and the resolution time — and it is not named in the query, which is the capability under test. However, the remaining two added columns (requires_external_action, external_dependency_type) are mediators/downstream states on the causal pathway rather than confounders, so they do not support de-confounding and would in fact bias a naive adjustment. With only a single valid confounder and no adjustment for pre-treatment common causes like ticket priority (already in the original table) or agent skill, confounder coverage is thin. The intervention is set up, but the confounding control is weak."
}
```

